skill(port-daddy-agent): pd-shim, roadmap pop, continuous coordination

Three gaps surfaced this week in actual agent operation that the skill
didn't cover:

1. The pd-shim (`~/.port-daddy/bin/git`) intercepts destructive git
   verbs (reset --hard, rebase, stash push, etc.) and consults the
   coordination guard. Agents were treating shim refusals as bugs and
   reaching for `PD_SHIM_OFF=1`. The right move is to coordinate — read
   the refusal, salvage dead-session claims, wait on live ones, or DM
   the holder.

2. `pd roadmap pop` is the canonical "what's next" verb but wasn't in
   the CLI reference at all. ADR-0033 gives the atomicity contract
   (partial UNIQUE index on slug WHERE released_at IS NULL) and
   ADR-0034 wires session + agent onto the claim row via `--begin`.

3. Coordination is continuous, not a session-start ritual. Sessions
   TTL out, claims expire, other agents move while you work. Re-anchor
   before every commit/push/rebase, before pulling against main, and
   after any long-running build. The cost of a redundant `pd begin` is
   zero; the cost of pushing past a stale claim is real.

Plus a small pointer to the ADR-first → PR-α/β/γ slicing pattern that
this session's roadmap-schema and pd-backup PRs both follow.

